WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
Welcome. This class is about deciding
whether an agent's result

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:07.500
deserves acceptance. We will use the work
order, source material,

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.250
tool records, and observed outcomes to
make that decision. A

4
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.000
persuasive summary is a starting point for
review, not a

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.750
substitute for evidence. You will create a
criterion-to-evidence matrix, distinguish

6
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.500
a defect from missing evidence, and choose
accept, request changes,

7
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.250
or escalate. Our example is a support
agent asked to

8
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.000
summarize a policy and prepare a ticket
update. The update

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.750
must remain within the approved account
and must not be

10
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.500
sent without the required approval. The
policy summary must cite

11
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.250
the current source. Keep these
requirements visible as you examine

12
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:45.000
each artifact.

13
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.786
Start with the original work order. Write
the requested outcome,

14
00:00:48.786 --> 00:00:52.571
scope, constraints, and acceptance
criteria before reading the agent's

15
00:00:52.571 --> 00:00:56.357
completion claim. This order matters
because a polished answer can

16
00:00:56.357 --> 00:01:00.143
draw attention toward what the agent did
and away from

17
00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:03.929
what it omitted. Give each criterion its
own row. Record

18
00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:07.714
the artifact or postcondition that would
demonstrate success, the evidence

19
00:01:07.714 --> 00:01:11.500
location, the observed result, and the
review status. For our

20
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.286
support task, separate factual accuracy,
account isolation, approval, and delivery

21
00:01:15.286 --> 00:01:19.071
status. An accurate summary does not
compensate for an unauthorized

22
00:01:19.071 --> 00:01:22.857
update. Likewise, a valid approval does
not make a stale

23
00:01:22.857 --> 00:01:26.643
policy current. If the work order itself
is ambiguous, record

24
00:01:26.643 --> 00:01:30.429
the ambiguity and identify who can resolve
it. Do not

25
00:01:30.429 --> 00:01:34.214
silently replace a difficult requirement
with one that is easier

26
00:01:34.214 --> 00:01:38.000
to satisfy.

27
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Trace material claims to evidence that
supports their exact wording

28
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.000
and scope. Open the cited policy, check
its date and

29
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.000
applicability, and locate the passage
behind the claim. A working

30
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.000
URL proves only that a page can be
reached. Distinguish

31
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
an observed fact from an inference and
keep uncertainty visible.

32
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.000
For an action, inspect the authorized
identity, operation arguments, approval

33
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.000
record, execution result, and actual
postcondition in the system of

34
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
record. A timeout after a write leaves the
outcome unknown:

35
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
it does not prove that nothing happened.
Use a durable

36
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
operation identifier to reconcile that
state before recommending a retry.

37
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000
For code, inspect the changed artifact and
reproduce the relevant

38
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
check against the exact revision. A test
report from an

39
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
earlier revision cannot establish the
behavior of a later change.

40
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Keep secrets and personal information out
of the review packet.

41
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.929
Use three review outcomes deliberately.
Accept when every required criterion

42
00:02:37.929 --> 00:02:41.857
has supporting evidence and remaining risk
is within the agreed

43
00:02:41.857 --> 00:02:45.786
boundary. Request changes when you can
describe a reproducible defect

44
00:02:45.786 --> 00:02:49.714
or a specific missing artifact that the
author can supply.

45
00:02:49.714 --> 00:02:53.643
Escalate when deciding requires authority
you do not have, the

46
00:02:53.643 --> 00:02:57.571
external outcome cannot yet be determined,
or the possible impact

47
00:02:57.571 --> 00:03:01.500
exceeds your review scope. Do not average
away a failed

48
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.429
mandatory criterion with several
successful ones. A useful decision names

49
00:03:05.429 --> 00:03:09.357
the affected requirement, the evidence,
its practical consequence, and the

50
00:03:09.357 --> 00:03:13.286
next check needed. Keep the original
result and findings so

51
00:03:13.286 --> 00:03:17.214
a revision can be compared honestly. When
the author returns,

52
00:03:17.214 --> 00:03:21.143
check the changed criteria and any
connected behavior that the

53
00:03:21.143 --> 00:03:25.071
change could affect. Record what was
retested instead of implying

54
00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:29.000
that an entire system was requalified.

55
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.867
Consider our support agent's report:
policy summarized, customer notified, all

56
00:03:32.867 --> 00:03:36.733
checks passed. Open the artifacts before
accepting those claims. The

57
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:40.600
summary cites an older policy revision, so
mark the current-policy

58
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:44.467
criterion failed and identify the source
that must replace it.

59
00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:48.333
The approval record authorizes a draft
only, while the trace

60
00:03:48.333 --> 00:03:52.200
reports a send attempt. Mark the
authorization criterion failed and

61
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.067
escalate the possible external effect. The
send request timed out,

62
00:03:56.067 --> 00:03:59.933
so customer notification is unknown until
the message system is

63
00:03:59.933 --> 00:04:03.800
queried using the operation identifier.
Finally, the test log covers

64
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.667
formatting but never checks the account
boundary. Mark account-isolation evidence

65
00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:11.533
unknown, not passed. These rows produce a
request for corrections

66
00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:15.400
plus an escalation for the potentially
unauthorized action. The summary's

67
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.267
confident wording changes none of those
findings. This scenario is

68
00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:23.133
a classroom fixture; use synthetic records
and do not send

69
00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:27.000
a real message.

70
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.923
Now build your own matrix from the
fixture. Start with

71
00:04:30.923 --> 00:04:34.846
four rows: current policy, authorized
action, correct account, and observed

72
00:04:34.846 --> 00:04:38.769
delivery status. For each, name the
smallest check that would

73
00:04:38.769 --> 00:04:42.692
resolve its state. Separate the evidence
you already have from

74
00:04:42.692 --> 00:04:46.615
evidence you are requesting. A policy page
and its revision

75
00:04:46.615 --> 00:04:50.538
can resolve the factual claim. A scoped
test with two

76
00:04:50.538 --> 00:04:54.462
synthetic accounts can examine the account
boundary. A delivery record

77
00:04:54.462 --> 00:04:58.385
can establish whether the message exists,
but it cannot retroactively

78
00:04:58.385 --> 00:05:02.308
authorize a send. Write one sentence
explaining that distinction. If

79
00:05:02.308 --> 00:05:06.231
you discover a criterion missing from the
original work order,

80
00:05:06.231 --> 00:05:10.154
record it as a proposed clarification
rather than pretending it

81
00:05:10.154 --> 00:05:14.077
was always required. Pause here and
compare your matrix with

82
00:05:14.077 --> 00:05:18.000
a partner or the supplied feedback.

83
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.750
Before making the decision, answer this
question: can a result

84
00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.500
be accepted because every automated test
passed while one mandatory

85
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.250
source citation is unsupported? Explain
which criterion remains unverified and

86
00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:33.000
what evidence would change your decision.
Then consider a second

87
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.750
case: the system of record confirms
delivery, but the approval

88
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.500
permitted only drafting. Delivery is now
an observed fact; authorization

89
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.250
still failed. Your review must preserve
both conclusions. A successful

90
00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:48.000
side effect and a permitted side effect
are different checks.

91
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.750
Finally, decide who should receive the
escalation for a possibly

92
00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:55.500
unauthorized action in your organization.
Name the responsible role rather

93
00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.250
than inventing permission to investigate
or reverse an external action

94
00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:03.000
yourself.

95
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.846
If you withheld acceptance because the
citation did not support

96
00:06:06.846 --> 00:06:10.692
the mandatory claim, your decision follows
the work order. Ask

97
00:06:10.692 --> 00:06:14.538
for a supported correction and then check
the revised source

98
00:06:14.538 --> 00:06:18.385
relationship. If you accepted because the
tests passed, identify what

99
00:06:18.385 --> 00:06:22.231
those tests actually exercised. They may
prove formatting, parsing, or

100
00:06:22.231 --> 00:06:26.077
a particular function, while saying
nothing about source currency. If

101
00:06:26.077 --> 00:06:29.923
you treated confirmed delivery as
sufficient authorization, revisit the

102
00:06:29.923 --> 00:06:33.769
approval record. Verification tells us
what happened; authorization tells us

103
00:06:33.769 --> 00:06:37.615
what was allowed. Keep those columns
separate in the matrix.

104
00:06:37.615 --> 00:06:41.462
Escalation should include the observed
facts, unresolved questions, potential

105
00:06:41.462 --> 00:06:45.308
impact, and the decision required. It
should not convert a

106
00:06:45.308 --> 00:06:49.154
hypothesis into an accusation or conceal
uncertainty behind a vague

107
00:06:49.154 --> 00:06:53.000
statement that the agent failed.

108
00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:56.714
Open the audit activity. Use a synthetic
or appropriately redacted

109
00:06:56.714 --> 00:07:00.429
work order, result, sources, tool trace,
and test report. Reproduce

110
00:07:00.429 --> 00:07:04.143
one check, inspect two material sources,
and verify one action

111
00:07:04.143 --> 00:07:07.857
against the system of record or its
classroom fixture. Submit

112
00:07:07.857 --> 00:07:11.571
your matrix and decision record with exact
evidence references. A

113
00:07:11.571 --> 00:07:15.286
reviewer should be able to repeat your
reasoning without trusting

114
00:07:15.286 --> 00:07:19.000
the agent's summary.

115
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:23.000
Begin the knowledge check when you can
explain what defines

116
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:27.000
completion, how to investigate an unknown
write outcome, why a

117
00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.000
citation must support the exact claim, and
when evidence is

118
00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:35.000
sufficient for acceptance. Return to your
matrix if any of

119
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.000
those decisions still depends on the agent
saying it finished.

120
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:42.500
Review starts with the work order and ends
with a

121
00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:46.000
decision another person can verify.
Preserve the distinction between facts,

122
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.500
defects, and missing evidence. Accept
demonstrated completion, request precise

123
00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:53.000
corrections, and escalate unresolved
authority or impact. The result is

124
00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:56.500
a trustworthy review record, even when the
agent's delivery is

125
00:10:56.500 --> 00:11:00.000
not yet ready.
